Nautilus DevOps: Copying an Encrypted File to a Docker Container
This document details the procedure, steps, and validation results for copying the encrypted file /tmp/nautilus.txt.gpg from App Server 2 (stapp02) into the running ubuntu_latest container without modifying its contents.

1. Overview & Objectives
Target Server: Application Server 2 (stapp02 / IP: 10.244.30.5)

Source File: /tmp/nautilus.txt.gpg

Target Destination: /opt/ inside the running Docker container named ubuntu_latest

Constraint: Ensure the file integrity is maintained (verified via SHA-256 checksum).

2. Implementation Steps
Step 2.1: Access App Server 2
SSH into Application Server 2 from the jump host using the provided credentials:

Bash
ssh steve@stapp02
(Password: Am3ric@)

Step 2.2: Verify Original File Checksum
Calculate the SHA-256 checksum of the source file on the host to establish a baseline:

Bash
sha256sum /tmp/nautilus.txt.gpg
Step 2.3: Copy the File to the Container
Use the docker cp utility to copy the file directly into the target container at /opt/:

Bash
docker cp /tmp/nautilus.txt.gpg ubuntu_latest:/opt/
Step 2.4: Verify File Integrity Inside the Container
Inspect the file permissions and calculate the checksum of the copied file inside the container to ensure zero modification:

Bash
docker exec ubuntu_latest ls -l /opt/nautilus.txt.gpg
docker exec ubuntu_latest sha256sum /opt/nautilus.txt.gpg
